statement:
Let $n\ge2$. In the plane, $n$ segments are given so that every two segments intersect at an interior point, and no three segments pass through one point. Jeff places a snail at one endpoint of each segment. Whenever he claps, every snail moves along its segment to the next intersection point and waits there. After $n-1$ claps, every snail reaches the intersection point farthest from its starting endpoint.

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if $n$ is odd, Jeff can always choose the starting endpoints so that no two snails ever occupy the same intersection point.
\item[(b)] Prove that if $n$ is even, then for every choice of starting endpoints there is a moment when two snails occupy the same intersection point.
\end{enumerate}
